WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:03.340
- [Instructor] We are told
to write seven hundredths

00:00:03.340 --> 00:00:06.260
as a fraction and a decimal.

00:00:06.260 --> 00:00:08.380
Why don't you get some
paper and a pencil out

00:00:08.380 --> 00:00:11.530
and see if you can do that
before we do it together.

00:00:11.530 --> 00:00:14.150
All right, so let's do
it first as a fraction.

00:00:14.150 --> 00:00:17.450
So what is going to be the
denominator of our fraction

00:00:17.450 --> 00:00:20.200
if they're saying seven hundredths?

00:00:20.200 --> 00:00:22.130
And the way I'm saying it
is a little bit of a hint.

00:00:22.130 --> 00:00:25.530
Seven hundredths, oh, I
think you got the picture.

00:00:25.530 --> 00:00:27.480
We're dealing with hundredths.

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:30.600
So our denominator is going to be 100.

00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:34.140
And then how many hundredths do we have?

00:00:34.140 --> 00:00:35.580
Well we have seven of them.

00:00:35.580 --> 00:00:37.270
I'll do that in a different
color just to be clear.

00:00:37.270 --> 00:00:39.840
We have seven of those hundredths.

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:42.520
So there you have it, seven hundredths.

00:00:42.520 --> 00:00:46.080
That's this expressed as a fraction.

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:49.030
Now what about as a decimal?

00:00:49.030 --> 00:00:52.380
Well, we could think
about our decimal places.

00:00:52.380 --> 00:00:56.180
If let's say that this is the ones place,

00:00:56.180 --> 00:00:57.670
and I'm just putting a little blank here.

00:00:57.670 --> 00:00:58.520
So this is the ones place,

00:00:58.520 --> 00:01:00.400
and you have a decimal right over here.

00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:03.550
And then this would be the tenths place.

00:01:03.550 --> 00:01:07.880
And then this would be
the hundredths place.

00:01:07.880 --> 00:01:11.220
Well we have, we want to
represent seven hundredths.

00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:14.150
So let me be clear, this
right over here is ones.

00:01:14.150 --> 00:01:19.150
This is tenths, and this is hundredths.

00:01:19.300 --> 00:01:23.500
I like saying, it's unusually
fun to say that, hundredths.

00:01:23.500 --> 00:01:25.360
All right, ones, tenths, hundredths.

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:26.870
So how many ones do we have here?

00:01:26.870 --> 00:01:29.410
Well we have no ones, we have zero ones.

00:01:29.410 --> 00:01:30.900
How many tenths do we have here?

00:01:30.900 --> 00:01:32.900
Well we have no tenths.

00:01:32.900 --> 00:01:35.350
How many hundredths do we have?

00:01:35.350 --> 00:01:37.700
Well we have seven hundredths.

00:01:37.700 --> 00:01:38.990
Okay now it's getting annoying.

00:01:38.990 --> 00:01:40.600
We have seven hundredths.

00:01:40.600 --> 00:01:41.890
So you can write it that way as well.

00:01:41.890 --> 00:01:43.620
And if I wanted to just write
it a little bit cleaner,

00:01:43.620 --> 00:01:48.620
I could just write no ones, no
tenths, and seven hundredths.

00:01:49.250 --> 00:01:52.090
I said it the last time
like a normal person.

00:01:52.090 --> 00:01:53.823
Let's do another example.

00:01:56.570 --> 00:02:01.570
Here, we're told select the
written form of each number.

00:02:01.570 --> 00:02:04.640
And so they, on the left right over here,

00:02:04.640 --> 00:02:06.250
you have different representations here.

00:02:06.250 --> 00:02:09.110
We have things written
as a decimal, a fraction,

00:02:09.110 --> 00:02:11.230
another decimal, and
then we want to say hey,

00:02:11.230 --> 00:02:13.800
which of these are represented in words

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:15.780
or a combination of
numbers and words up here.

00:02:15.780 --> 00:02:19.070
So pause this video and
have a go at this as well.

00:02:19.070 --> 00:02:21.470
Okay, so this first
number right over here,

00:02:21.470 --> 00:02:24.310
we have no ones, and
then as we go one space

00:02:24.310 --> 00:02:27.200
to the right of the decimal,
this is the tenths place.

00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:29.830
And it's clear we have
four of those tenths.

00:02:29.830 --> 00:02:32.960
So this right over here is four tenths.

00:02:32.960 --> 00:02:36.270
So that is this choice
right here, so we would,

00:02:36.270 --> 00:02:38.440
I'll shade it in, if you're
doing this on Khan Academy,

00:02:38.440 --> 00:02:41.090
you would just click there
and it would fill in.

00:02:41.090 --> 00:02:42.143
So what about this one?

00:02:42.143 --> 00:02:44.030
Well this one, we would read,

00:02:44.030 --> 00:02:47.610
you have four out of ten, or four tenths.

00:02:47.610 --> 00:02:50.460
So this again would be four tenths.

00:02:50.460 --> 00:02:53.173
So we would shade that one in.

00:02:54.410 --> 00:02:56.180
Now what's going on over here?

00:02:56.180 --> 00:02:59.100
We have no ones, we have no tenths.

00:02:59.100 --> 00:03:00.790
But we have four hundredths.

00:03:00.790 --> 00:03:03.100
I said it again, it's too much fun.

00:03:03.100 --> 00:03:04.760
So we have four hundredths.

00:03:04.760 --> 00:03:06.380
So that's this column.

00:03:06.380 --> 00:03:10.243
So we would fill that
one in, and we're done.

